w3m: WWW wo Miru Tool
=====================

w3m is a pager with WWW capability.  It IS a pager, but it can be used
as a text-mode WWW browser.

This package is maintained for Debian <https://www.debian.org>,
forked from the original version <https://sourceforge.net/projects/w3m/>.

If you can read English, see doc/*.
If you can read Japanese, see doc-jp/*.
If you can read both, read both and correct English. :-)


w3m-vimium (this fork)
======================

This repository is a fork of w3m that adds a comprehensive Vimium/Qutebrowser-style
workflow and keyboard-driven efficiency.

Added features
--------------

- Link hint mode & URL copying:
  - `f` hint visible links and follow in current buffer
  - `F` hint visible links and open in a new tab
  - `yf` hint visible links and copy the selected link URL to clipboard
  - `yF` / `ym` hint visible links and copy Markdown link (`[Title](URL)`) to clipboard
  - `yi` hint visible images and copy the selected image URL to clipboard
  - `yd` / `df` hint visible links and download target in background
  - `gy` copy link URL under cursor to clipboard (or current page URL if not on link)
  - `gY` copy Markdown link (`[Title](URL)`) under cursor to clipboard
- Yank / copy (`y` prefix):
  - `yy` copy current page URL to clipboard
  - `yp` / `yT` copy current page title to clipboard
  - `ya` copy all hyperlink URLs on current page to clipboard (line-separated list)
  - `yA` / `yM` copy all hyperlink URLs on current page as a Markdown link list (`- [Title](URL)`)
  - `yc` / `yC` yank current or nearest code block directly to clipboard (auto-strips line numbers)
  - `yt` duplicate current tab in a new tab
- Navigation & Pagination:
  - `]]` / `[[` automatically find and follow Next / Previous pagination links
  - `]a` / `[a` (also `g+` / `g-`) increment / decrement numbers in the current URL
  - `]h` / `[h` (also `gj` / `gk`) jump to next / previous heading on page
  - `]c` / `[c` (also `gc` / `gC`) jump to next / previous code block
  - `*` / `#` search word under cursor backward / forward
  - `gO` / `go` open interactive hierarchical Table of Contents (outline popup)
- Paste and navigate:
  - `p` open URL from clipboard in current buffer
  - `P` open URL from clipboard in a new tab
- Smart Vomnibar & search:
  - `b` / `o` open interactive Vomnibar (multi-token fuzzy search across open tabs, bookmarks, history, and search engines)
  - `B` / `O` open Vomnibar in a new tab
  - `t` open URL or search query in a new tab
  - `ga` / `gb` instant bookmark current page / view bookmarks
  - Smart search engine prefixes: `g` (Google), `gh` (GitHub), `gl` (GitLab), `w` (Wikipedia), `b` (Bilibili), `z` (Zhihu), `yt` (YouTube), `d` (DuckDuckGo), `x` (Twitter/X), `bd` (Baidu), `v2` (V2EX), `so` (StackOverflow), `crates` (Rust Crates), `npm` (NPM), `brew` (Homebrew), `hn` (Hacker News), `py` (PyPI), `mdn` (MDN Web Docs), `go` (Go Packages), `hf` (Hugging Face), `arxiv` (ArXiv), `bing` (Bing)
- Tab management:
  - `x` close current tab (records closed tab to undo stack)
  - `X` restore the most recently closed tab
  - `J` / `K` (also `gt` / `gT`, `{` / `}`, `]t` / `[t`) switch to next / previous tab
  - `<<` / `>>` (also `g<` / `g>`, `[T` / `]T`) move current tab left / right
  - `gxa` close all other tabs (keep current)
  - `gx$` close all tabs to the right
  - `gx0` close all tabs to the left
  - `g0` / `g$` jump to first / last tab
  - `yt` duplicate current tab in a new tab
- Form & Mark navigation:
  - `gi` focus first/next editable text input (supports count prefix `[count]gi` to jump to N-th input)
  - `ge` open Neovim/external editor for form textarea/input under cursor
  - `m<a-z>` set local position mark
  - `'<a-z>` jump to local position mark
- Vimium-like navigation remaps (default keymap):
  - `j` scroll down, `k` scroll up
  - `h` scroll left, `l` scroll right
  - `d` scroll down half page, `u` scroll up half page
  - `H` back (previous buffer in stack), `L` forward (next buffer)
  - `gg` go to top (first line), `G` go to bottom (last line)
  - `?` show help panel

Clipboard integration
---------------------

Cross-platform clipboard read/write uses the first available tool:

- **macOS**: `pbcopy` / `pbpaste`
- **Wayland (Linux)**: `wl-copy` / `wl-paste`
- **X11 (Linux/BSD)**: `xclip` / `xsel`
- **Windows (WSL / Cygwin)**: `clip.exe` / `putclip` / `getclip` / `powershell`

You can customize clipboard commands via environment variables:

    export W3M_CLIPBOARD_CMD="pbcopy"          # Writes text from stdin
    export W3M_CLIPBOARD_PASTE_CMD="pbpaste"   # Outputs text to stdout

If no clipboard tool is available, clipboard commands will display an informative message.

Build / install notes
---------------------

w3m requires Boehm GC headers (`gc.h`). You can either install your distro's GC
development package, or build/install Boehm GC yourself and point w3m to it.

On Fedora, you may need to explicitly select terminfo at configure time:

    --with-termlib=:libtinfo.so.6

Upstream / remotes
------------------

- `origin` points to this fork.
- `upstream` points to the upstream w3m repository.
